\section{A user-level estimator of the water footprint of LLM conversations}

The mitigation strategies discussed above are largely implemented at the level of data-centre design, hardware development, model optimisation and infrastructure management, where individual users have little direct control over the efficiency with which a request is eventually processed.  The environmental impact of a digital service, however, also depends on the scale and intensity with which that service is used: every query represents a small additional computational workload, while subscriptions, repeated interactions and the adoption of increasingly demanding functionalities contribute collectively to the economic demand that sustains the continued expansion of AI infrastructure.  From this perspective, the individual user occupies an unusual position, simultaneously acting as the consumer of the service and as one of the elementary sources of the aggregate workload whose growth ultimately determines future resource requirements.  This consideration motivated us to complement the infrastructure-level discussion with a simple user-oriented estimator, designed to translate an ordinary LLM conversation into an approximate operational water footprint.

The proposed website accepts a conversation exported or copied from the web interfaces of the principal consumer LLM providers, including ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Grok and DeepSeek.  The user is asked only to specify the model employed and the level of reasoning or ``thinking'' selected during the conversation, while all remaining information is reconstructed automatically from the visible chat history.  In particular, the parser identifies the ordered sequence of user and assistant messages, counts the tokens contained in each of them, reconstructs the visible context preceding every generated answer and records the occurrence of observable auxiliary operations such as web searches, code execution, image interpretation, image generation and video generation.  Quantities that are inaccessible to an ordinary user, including the exact serving hardware, internal system prompts, cache utilisation, hidden routing operations and physical data-centre location, are deliberately excluded from the conversation-specific input rather than being inferred from information that the user cannot verify.

The central approximation of the estimator is that the computational demand of text generation can be reconstructed from the number of tokens processed and generated during the conversation.  For the $i$-th assistant response, the visible input length $N_i^{\rm in}$ is obtained by counting all messages preceding that response along the visible conversation branch, whereas $N_i^{\rm out}$ corresponds to the tokens contained in the resulting answer.  This distinction becomes increasingly relevant in long conversations because later requests are generally conditioned on a substantial part of the previous exchange, meaning that the computational workload grows faster than the number of tokens visible in the final transcript alone.  Input and output tokens are assigned different energy coefficients because prompt processing can be parallelised efficiently, whereas autoregressive decoding requires the sequential production of output tokens; measurements of LLM inference consistently identify decoding as a dominant contribution for ordinary prompts, while also showing that long contexts can make input processing progressively more significant~\cite{oviedo2026energy,argerich2024energy}.

Because the reasoning performed internally by proprietary systems is not generally preserved in consumer chat exports, the selected thinking level is used as a proxy for additional hidden generation.  We define an effective generated-token count $(1+r_\ell)N_i^{\rm out}$, with $r_\ell=0,1,3,7,$ and $14$ for standard, low, medium, high and maximum reasoning respectively.  These values should be interpreted as modelling priors rather than measurements of the internal reasoning trace; their scale is motivated by recent analyses of test-time computation, which show that reasoning-intensive requests can generate an order of magnitude more internal tokens than ordinary inference and can correspondingly increase energy consumption by more than one order of magnitude~\cite{oviedo2026energy}.  When applied to a simple web transcript, this approximation provides a transparent way of representing a computational contribution that would otherwise disappear entirely from the estimate.

The conversion from token counts to electricity requires a model-dependent coefficient.  Since sufficiently detailed energy measurements are not publicly available for every proprietary model, the website groups models into four broad efficiency classes rather than assigning an apparently precise physical constant to each commercial product.  The central output-generation coefficients are $0.15$, $0.50$, $1.00$ and $1.50$ Wh per thousand generated tokens for lightweight, standard, frontier and particularly compute-intensive models respectively, values chosen to span recent measurements and estimates ranging from efficient 70-billion-parameter models to modern frontier deployments~\cite{jegham2025hungry,oviedo2026energy}.  For an input context of length $L$, the input-token coefficient is taken as $10\%$, $20\%$ or $35\%$ of the corresponding output coefficient for $L\leq1000$, $1000<L\leq5000$ and $L>5000$ tokens.  This simple dependence preserves the observed increase in the importance of prompt processing for long contexts without requiring knowledge of the proprietary serving architecture.

Visible auxiliary operations are added separately because several of them cannot meaningfully be represented as text tokens.  A web search is assigned a central value of $0.04$ Wh, while a short code-execution event is assigned $0.01$ Wh; image interpretation contributes approximately $0.06$ Wh per image, whereas image generation is considerably more demanding, with a central estimate of $2.9$ Wh per generated image based on measurements across contemporary generative models~\cite{luccioni2024powerhungry}.  Video generation presents an even broader range of computational requirements, with recent benchmarks ranging from fractions of a Wh to more than $100$ Wh for a short generated clip depending on model size, spatial resolution and temporal length; in the absence of such technical information in ordinary conversation exports, the tool adopts a representative central value of $25$ Wh per visible video generation and reports this component with correspondingly large uncertainty~\cite{delavande2025video}.  Uploaded documents are not assigned an independent fixed surcharge because the processing required for a document can vary by orders of magnitude with its length, modality and the use of OCR or retrieval, while any document text that becomes part of the visible conversational context is already reflected in the token count.

With these contributions, the estimated electricity associated with a conversation is written as
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
E_{\rm chat}=\sum_i \Big[&
e^{\rm in}_{m_i}(N_i^{\rm in})N_i^{\rm in}
+e^{\rm out}_{m_i}(1+r_\ell)N_i^{\rm out}\\
&+0.04n_i^{\rm web}
+0.01n_i^{\rm code}
+0.06n_i^{\rm img,in}\\
&+2.9n_i^{\rm img,out}
+25n_i^{\rm video}
\Big],
\end{split}
\label{eq:chat-energy}
\end{equation}
where energies are expressed in Wh and the $n_i$ denote the number of visible auxiliary operations associated with the $i$-th response.

The final conversion from electricity to water preserves the distinction introduced in the previous sections between water consumed directly at the data centre and water associated with electricity production.  Recent sustainability disclosures from large cloud operators indicate direct water-use intensities of the order of a few tenths of a litre per kWh for highly efficient facilities, although substantially higher values have also been reported for particular AI-serving fleets, while electricity-related consumption varies considerably with the generation mix.  For a location-independent estimator we therefore adopt a central direct-water factor of $0.2$ mL/Wh and, following the world-average electricity-mix factor used by EcoLogits, an indirect factor of $3.9$ mL/Wh~\cite{ecologits2025,elsworth2025gemini}.  The headline estimate is consequently
\begin{equation}
W_{\rm chat}\simeq4.1\,E_{\rm chat},
\label{eq:chat-water}
\end{equation}
with $W_{\rm chat}$ expressed in millilitres.  Direct and electricity-related contributions are displayed separately, together with a broad uncertainty interval reflecting the variability of model efficiency, hidden reasoning, auxiliary operations and the physical infrastructure on which the requests may have been executed.

The objective of the tool is therefore not to reconstruct the exact amount of water evaporated in the particular facility that served a conversation, information that remains inaccessible to an ordinary consumer, but to provide an interpretable estimate based exclusively on quantities that the user can either supply directly or recover from the conversation itself.  The resulting number can be understood in the same way as many other personal environmental indicators: its value lies primarily in making the scale of an otherwise invisible resource demand tangible, in revealing which behaviours contribute most strongly to it, and in allowing users to compare, for example, short textual interactions with reasoning-intensive exchanges or image- and video-generation workflows.  At the level of a single conversation the quantities involved may remain modest, yet the significance of such individual choices emerges from their repetition across a user base that now numbers hundreds of millions of people, linking the behaviour of the individual consumer to the aggregate demand that drives the continued expansion of AI infrastructure.
:::
